  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: Explain how relational database executes SQL query from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Complete sentences, avoid repeating. Need produce final comprehensive. Need think structure.\n\nNeed likely include: SQL input, lexical parsing, validation, semantic analysis, catalog, rewrite, logical plan, logical optimization, physical optimization, execution engine, row vs vectorized, indexing types B-tree, hash, covering, bitmap, BRIN, GIN/GiST, statistics, selectivity, join types nested loop, hash join, merge join, sort, aggregate, window, subqueries, CTEs, transactions ACID, MVCC, locking, isolation levels, deadlock, returning rows, network, client. Concrete examples: employees, orders, order_items, customers. SQL query. Show plans (EXPLAIN). Pitfalls: full scans, missing indexes, index on low selectivity, leading column, ORs, implicit casts, function on indexed column, index-only scan vs heap fetch, index bloat/vacuum, statistics stale, parameter sniffing, plan cache, cardinality misestimates, join order, fan-out, correlated subqueries, IN vs EXISTS, UNION vs UNION ALL, LIMIT misuse, large transactions, lock contention, long running read uncommitted, etc. Tradeoffs: indexes write overhead, storage, maintenance, optimizer cost; materialized views, caching; query rewriting; partitioning; read replicas; MVCC vs locking. Need avoid repetition. Complete sentences.\n\nNeed ensure no repeated content. Can be detailed tutorial. Use headings.\n\nNeed maybe clarify RDBMS generic. Use PostgreSQL examples? MySQL/SQL Server differences? Could say terms common; examples use PostgreSQL EXPLAIN. Include SQL Server/MySQL nuance maybe. Need technical.\n\nLet's design answer:\n\nTitle: How a Relational Database Executes a SQL Query\n\nIntro: When you submit SELECT/UPDATE, database does pipeline: front end, optimizer, executor, storage, transaction management.\n\n1. Receive query and transaction context\n- client connects, backend process/thread, session, autocommit or explicit transaction. Authentication. Statement parsed.\n\n2. Parsing and validation\n- lexical analysis, parsing to AST, name resolution using catalog/system tables, type checking, implicit casts, privileges. Example: SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.status='sh",
